# Two Kings Chess: Netlify. The published folder is the web export, which holds none of the
# repository's own files; these answer 404 for the ones that would be there if a checkout were
# published by mistake, and for Apache's config, which scripts/build-web.mjs leaves out of a
# --host netlify build anyway. The ! makes a rule apply even where a file exists (Netlify serves
# an existing file over a rule without it). Netlify serves dotfiles; .well-known/ is the one
# folder of them that belongs to the site. Cloudflare Pages has no 404 rules: build for it with
# --host cloudflare, which keeps only its own config.
/.htaccess        /404.html  404!
/.git/*           /404.html  404!
/.github/*        /404.html  404!
/.env             /404.html  404!
/README.md        /404.html  404!
/package.json     /404.html  404!
/deploy/*         /404.html  404!
/public/*         /404.html  404!
/src/*            /404.html  404!
